From f88c43d4cd17ec656216a262edefb5120019d1f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chandler Newman <chandler2newman@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:26:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] venus: snapshot a context and restore it in another
 process

A Vulkan driver's state cannot be read out, but every command that built a
Venus context passes through the decoder here. So each command whose effect
lasts -- object creation, memory binds, descriptor updates, command buffer
recording -- is kept as the raw bytes it arrived as, against the objects it
concerns, and dropped when they are destroyed -- unless a later command
names them, as a pipeline names the shader modules a program destroys as
soon as it has one. Replayed in their original
order through the same decoder, they recreate every object under the id the
guest already holds, since Venus ids are the guest's own. Submits, waits and
queries are not kept: their lasting effect is on memory, which is read back.

vkr_record_dispatch wraps the three places commands are dispatched -- context
submission, ring threads, and nested command streams -- and handlers note the
objects their command concerns where the arguments do not say it plainly.

A snapshot drains every ring, idles every device, and writes the recorded
commands, each ring's virtqueue seqno, every host-visible allocation's
contents and the state of fences, events and semaphores. A binary
semaphore's payload has no query, so the submit and semaphore handlers keep
it. A restore replays the commands with replies switched off -- nobody is
waiting for them -- then writes memory back and signals what was signalled.
A ring created during a restore resumes at the head its shared memory holds
rather than refusing a non-zero one, and starts only once the rest is back.

It reaches the render server as two context ops, SNAPSHOT and RESTORE, which
carry the snapshot as a file descriptor, and the embedder as
virgl_renderer_context_snapshot and virgl_renderer_context_restore.
